    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1544.8705642108764,
      "y": 87.94469102914172,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 777,
      "versionNonce": 1798578925,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766655056064,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Handling video processing to support adaptive bitrate streaming.\n- Split up the original file into segments (using a tool like ffmpeg or similar).\nThese segments will be transcoded (converted from one encoding to\nanother) and used to generated different video containers.\n- Transcode (convert from one encoding to another) each segment and\nprocess other aspects of the segments (audio, transcript generation).\n- Create manifest files referencing the different segments in different video\nformats. Mark the upload as \"complete\".\n\n2) Resumable uploads:\n- Video is split into small chunks (about 5-10 MB), each with a unique\nfingerprint hash.\n- Video metadata stores a list of chunks, each with its fingerprint and a\nstatus (e.g., NotUploaded or Uploaded).\n- The client first sends this chunk list to the backend with all chunk\nstatuses set to NotUploaded.\n- The client uploads each chunk to Amazon S3; S3 returns a part number\nand ETag for each uploaded part.\n- The client sends part number and ETag to the backend, which verifies\nthem with S3 and marks the matching chunk as Uploaded.\n- After all chunks are uploaded, the client calls CompleteMultipartUpload; S3\nemits a single object-level event that can trigger further processing.\n- If the upload is interrupted, the client can resume by reading\nVideoMetadata and only re-uploading chunks that are not yet marked as\nUploaded.\n\n3) scaling to handle large number of video uploaded/watched a day.\n- video service: its stateless we do horizontal scaling\n- video Metadata: horizontal scaling.\n- video processing svc: queue mechanism to handle burst of video uploads.\n- adding a cache to fetch videos metadata- specifically for popular video\nLRU/LFU.\n- we can further introduce a CDN. it will cache popular videos.\n\n4) Speeding up uploads: clients can segment the video and upload them\ninstead of uploading the entire video. Backend can do the post processing\non those segments and upload to s3.\n\n5) Resuming streaming where the user left off requires storing more data\nper user per video.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Handling video processing to support adaptive bitrate streaming.\n- Split up the original file into segments (using a tool like ffmpeg or similar). These segments will be transcoded (converted from one encoding to another) and used to generated different video containers.\n- Transcode (convert from one encoding to another) each segment and process other aspects of the segments (audio, transcript generation).\n- Create manifest files referencing the different segments in different video formats. Mark the upload as \"complete\".\n\n2) Resumable uploads:\n- Video is split into small chunks (about 5-10 MB), each with a unique fingerprint hash.\n- Video metadata stores a list of chunks, each with its fingerprint and a status (e.g., NotUploaded or Uploaded).\n- The client first sends this chunk list to the backend with all chunk statuses set to NotUploaded.\n- The client uploads each chunk to Amazon S3; S3 returns a part number and ETag for each uploaded part.\n- The client sends part number and ETag to the backend, which verifies them with S3 and marks the matching chunk as Uploaded.\n- After all chunks are uploaded, the client calls CompleteMultipartUpload; S3 emits a single object-level event that can trigger further processing.\n- If the upload is interrupted, the client can resume by reading VideoMetadata and only re-uploading chunks that are not yet marked as Uploaded.\n\n3) scaling to handle large number of video uploaded/watched a day.\n- video service: its stateless we do horizontal scaling\n- video Metadata: horizontal scaling.\n- video processing svc: queue mechanism to handle burst of video uploads.\n- adding a cache to fetch videos metadata- specifically for popular video LRU/LFU.\n- we can further introduce a CDN. it will cache popular videos.\n\n4) Speeding up uploads: clients can segment the video and upload them instead of uploading the entire video. Backend can do the post processing on those segments and upload to s3.\n\n5) Resuming streaming where the user left off requires storing more data per user per video.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
