    {
      "id": "z03bZSTUx09FPU5fSIbDc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 830.913113734093,
      "y": 1100.0351318753328,
      "width": 3530.122314453125,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bFt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1774534189,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 333902147,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1766654870829,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Storing video: uploading video with presigned url has been already covered in google drive.\n\nbad solution: raw video uploaded. As different devices require different video formats in order to play back video.\n\nGood Solution: using a video processing service to upload back raw video uploaded on s3 in various formats. Uploading entire video poses challenge for the next requirement of streaming video.\n\ngreat solution: using a video processing service uploading video in different formats as small chunks(segments).\n\nwatching video:\n\nbad solution: download entire video on the client side and then watching. Issues: huge wait time, if network distruption is there the download fails.\n\nGood solution: The client would choose a video format based on the user's device, bandwidth, and preferences and then go on to download segments instead of entire video. this way segments can be streamed along the way. Issues: doesnt account for flactuating network constraints while a user is watching a video.\n\nGreat Solution: Adaptive bitrate streaming. This is an upgradation to the previous approach. Uses the manifest file which has references to all the video segments that are available in different formats. If there is a slowing down of network the client will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any interruption in streaming.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Storing video: uploading video with presigned url has been already covered in google drive.\n\nbad solution: raw video uploaded. As different devices require different video formats in order to play back video.\n\nGood Solution: using a video processing service to upload back raw video uploaded on s3 in various formats. Uploading entire video poses challenge for the next requirement of streaming video.\n\ngreat solution: using a video processing service uploading video in different formats as small chunks(segments).\n\nwatching video:\n\nbad solution: download entire video on the client side and then watching. Issues: huge wait time, if network distruption is there the download fails.\n\nGood solution: The client would choose a video format based on the user's device, bandwidth, and preferences and then go on to download segments instead of entire video. this way segments can be streamed along the way. Issues: doesnt account for flactuating network constraints while a user is watching a video.\n\nGreat Solution: Adaptive bitrate streaming. This is an upgradation to the previous approach. Uses the manifest file which has references to all the video segments that are available in different formats. If there is a slowing down of network the client will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any interruption in streaming.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "T72U4AR3Wz2dqHtxhG3fe",
